 ## Overview
-Voxel volume rendering visualizes a 3D scalar field by projecting light absorption and emission through a discrete voxel grid, revealing internal density variations without surface extraction [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It differs from isosurface methods by rendering the entire volume data rather than a single thresholded boundary, and from analytic methods by operating on pre-computed discrete samples [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The method supports two distinct visual styles: "block mode," which snaps raymarching to voxel centers for a crisp, discrete appearance, and "smooth compositing," which interpolates values for a continuous look [S3, unofficial].
+Voxel volume rendering visualizes a 3D scalar field by projecting light absorption and emission through a discrete voxel grid, revealing internal density variations without surface extraction [S2, unofficial]. It differs from isosurface methods by rendering the entire volume data rather than a single thresholded boundary, and from analytic methods by operating on pre-computed discrete samples [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The method supports two distinct visual styles: "block mode," which snaps raymarching to voxel centers for a crisp, discrete appearance, and "smooth compositing," which interpolates values for a continuous look [S3, unofficial].
 
